## OP-1077 | [SP-B-X-019] Bridge heartbeat cadence independent of event-loop (OP-1067 follow-up) | 承認済み
LABELS: adr:0035,agent:auto,area:backend,area:tests,claim:default:1778702217450298-b6268efa,claim:default:1778702738407739-2534964c,class:subscription-codex,hot-fix,parent:SP-B-X-META,prefer:codex,priority:high,schema:v0,scope:sprint-s12-g,scope:sprint-sp-b-x,ticket-id:sp-b-x-019,tier:S,type:feature
# SP-B-X-019 — Bridge heartbeat cadence independent of event-loop (OP-1067 follow-up)

**Parent**: OP-1058 SP-B-X-META
**Hot-fix path**: operator-authorized A route — claude pushed Gerrit directly. Paper trail.

## Why this ticket

SP-B-X-009 / OP-1067 added bridge heartbeat emission so the runner-side
pickup gate can detect a stalled bridge. The implementation lives in
`backend/agents/gerrit_jira_bridge.py:1485-1489` inside `_maintenance_ticks()`:

```python
if now - self._last_heartbeat_file >= self.config.heartbeat_file_seconds:
    self._touch_heartbeat_file()
    self._last_heartbeat_file = now
```

`_maintenance_ticks()` runs INSIDE the event-loop iteration body, AFTER
`_dispatch_event_loop()` reads each event. The SSH `gerrit stream-events`
read is BLOCKING. During quiet periods (no Gerrit activity), the loop
sits in the SSH read for minutes → `_maintenance_ticks()` does not run →
heartbeat file goes stale → runner pickup gate fires "bridge_down" →
**every runner pickup is blocked**.

## Observed

2026-05-14 03:47+ (after the bridge daemon was restarted with the new
heartbeat code from OP-1067 deployed): the heartbeat file was written
ONCE at startup, then aged 30s → 60s → 90s → 130s+ over the next 2
minutes with NO further updates. Runner-side gate started blocking
pickups at the 5-min stale threshold. Worked around by running
`while true; do touch /var/run/omnisight-bridge/heartbeat; sleep 60; done`
as an external loop.

## Fix design

The maintenance pulse must NOT depend on event-loop progress. Two
candidate implementations:

**Option A — Background thread (simpler, recommended for hot-fix)**:
- Start a daemon thread in `Bridge.run()` startup that sleeps
  `heartbeat_file_seconds / 2` and calls `_touch_heartbeat_file()`.
- Thread exits on `Bridge.shutdown()`.
- Pros: minimal change; no event-loop refactor.
- Cons: shared state (counters, log handle) accessed from two threads —
  needs lock OR the heartbeat write must be self-contained (it
  currently is — only touches `path` from env).

**Option B — SSH stream read with timeout (more invasive)**:
- Replace `for line in proc.stdout:` blocking read with a select-based
  read that returns periodically with no data so the main loop body
  (including `_maintenance_ticks()`) gets to run.
- Pros: no second thread; pure single-loop design.
- Cons: bigger refactor; risks regressing the event-handling path.

This ticket implements **Option A**. Option B is a future cleanup if
the bridge ever grows other periodic concerns.

## Code AC

- [ ] New private method `Bridge._start_heartbeat_thread()` called from
  `Bridge.run()` early in startup (after log/config setup, before
  `_dispatch_event_loop`).
- [ ] Thread is `daemon=True` (dies with the process).
- [ ] Thread sleeps `max(5, heartbeat_file_seconds / 2)` between
  touches — half-cadence prevents stale-window straddling.
- [ ] Thread calls `_touch_heartbeat_file()`. Write errors are logged
  (existing behavior) and never raised.
- [ ] On `Bridge.shutdown()` (or KeyboardInterrupt), thread observes a
  shutdown flag and exits cleanly.
- [ ] The existing `_maintenance_ticks()` heartbeat path remains as a
  belt-and-suspenders second emission point; if Option B is ever
  implemented, both can coexist without duplicate writes.

## Tests

- `tests/test_bridge_heartbeat_thread.py`:
  - `test_heartbeat_thread_starts_on_run()` — patch `_dispatch_event_loop`
    to block forever; assert heartbeat file is touched within 2× cadence
    after `Bridge.run()` starts.
  - `test_heartbeat_thread_exits_on_shutdown()` — start bridge, signal
    shutdown, assert thread joins within timeout.
  - `test_heartbeat_thread_write_failure_does_not_crash()` — patch
    `touch_heartbeat_file` to raise; assert daemon stays alive.

## Operator action after merge

Restart bridge daemon to load the new code:

```bash
systemctl --user restart gerrit-jira-bridge.service
```

Then the manual touch loop at `/tmp/heartbeat-touch-loop.pid` can be
killed (`kill $(cat /tmp/heartbeat-touch-loop.pid)`).

## Boundaries

```yaml
loc_delta_max: 200
files_touched_max: 2
required_paths:
  - backend/agents/gerrit_jira_bridge.py
  - tests/test_bridge_heartbeat_thread.py
class: subscription-codex
authority_required: L3
tag_type: story
schema_version: v0
```



